WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.140 --> 00:00:01.670
- Halloween II would literally pick up

00:00:01.670 --> 00:00:03.070
right where the original left off,

00:00:03.070 --> 00:00:04.210
but that would not prevent it

00:00:04.210 --> 00:00:06.530
from subjecting the story
to some major changes,

00:00:06.530 --> 00:00:08.130
like some lore and motivation

00:00:08.130 --> 00:00:10.320
for the mysterious, masked antagonist.

00:00:10.320 --> 00:00:11.840
Police find the word Samhain,

00:00:11.840 --> 00:00:13.530
or as Loomis says it...

00:00:13.530 --> 00:00:14.363
- [Loomis] Sam Hain.

00:00:14.363 --> 00:00:15.970
- Written on a chalkboard
at the elementary school.

00:00:15.970 --> 00:00:17.900
The significance is only briefly explained

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:19.900
by Michael's psychiatrist, Dr. Loomis.

00:00:19.900 --> 00:00:21.340
He mentions that it's a Celtic word,

00:00:21.340 --> 00:00:22.300
meaning Lord of the Dead,

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:25.350
that the festival of Samhain
signified the end of summer.

00:00:25.350 --> 00:00:27.410
But the meaning and
connection to Michael Myers

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:29.180
and the overall message of Halloween II

00:00:29.180 --> 00:00:30.950
goes much deeper than that.

00:00:30.950 --> 00:00:32.710
It actually refers to a Gaelic festival,

00:00:32.710 --> 00:00:34.260
marking the end of the harvest season

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:36.530
and the beginning of the
darker half of the year.

00:00:36.530 --> 00:00:37.550
In the Northern hemisphere,

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:40.210
it is observed starting on
the evening of October 31st

00:00:40.210 --> 00:00:42.320
and ending on the evening of November 1st,

00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:44.380
much like Michael's rampage in this movie.

00:00:44.380 --> 00:00:45.620
It begins on Halloween night

00:00:45.620 --> 00:00:47.870
and carries over into the
first day of November.

00:00:47.870 --> 00:00:49.980
Early literature says that
Samhain was celebrated

00:00:49.980 --> 00:00:51.850
with the opening of ancient burial mounds,

00:00:51.850 --> 00:00:53.910
which were seen as portals
to the other world,

00:00:53.910 --> 00:00:56.720
which may have been opened
with bonfires and sacrifices.

00:00:56.720 --> 00:00:58.610
Maybe Myers' defeat at
the end of the movie

00:00:58.610 --> 00:01:01.530
is symbolic for its connection
to these Samhain festivals.

00:01:01.530 --> 00:01:03.020
It is believed that over time,

00:01:03.020 --> 00:01:05.330
Samhain merged with other
cultural celebrations,

00:01:05.330 --> 00:01:07.830
eventually becoming Halloween
as we know it today.

00:01:07.830 --> 00:01:09.370
So, it may come as no surprise

00:01:09.370 --> 00:01:10.560
that this word may be the key

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:12.200
to understanding everything else

00:01:12.200 --> 00:01:14.850
in the undertones of the
original Halloween sequel.

00:01:14.850 --> 00:01:16.140
For my entire analysis,

00:01:16.140 --> 00:01:18.440
stick around to the end of this video.

00:01:18.440 --> 00:01:20.705
This video is sponsored by BetterHelp.

00:01:20.705 --> 00:01:23.122
(rock music)

00:01:26.248 --> 00:01:27.081
(gun blasts)

00:01:27.081 --> 00:01:29.900
Six gunshots would not be enough
to keep Michael Myers down,

00:01:29.900 --> 00:01:31.580
just as dozens of angry mothers

00:01:31.580 --> 00:01:33.960
would not be enough to stop
me from making another episode

00:01:33.960 --> 00:01:35.180
of Things You Missed.

00:01:35.180 --> 00:01:36.140
Once again, I'm joined

00:01:36.140 --> 00:01:39.150
by YouTube's resident Halloween
expert, Jimmy Champane,

00:01:39.150 --> 00:01:41.667
or as Dr. Loomis would
pronounce it, Jimmy Sam Hain.

00:01:41.667 --> 00:01:43.150
- If that's how he says it,

00:01:43.150 --> 00:01:45.440
I don't even wanna
imagine how he'd spell it.

00:01:45.440 --> 00:01:47.800
- [Zac] Last time, I broke
down the psychosexual subtext

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:49.470
of the 1978 original.

00:01:49.470 --> 00:01:52.640
So in this episode, I'll
introspect the 1981 followup,

00:01:52.640 --> 00:01:54.720
Halloween II, the first of many attempts

00:01:54.720 --> 00:01:56.030
to create a direct sequel.

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:57.280
- Writer-director, John Carpenter,

00:01:57.280 --> 00:01:58.400
initially wasn't interested

00:01:58.400 --> 00:02:00.260
in continuing the story of Michael Myers,

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:02.200
but then he said, screw
it, I do like money.

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:04.890
So, he returned to produce
and write the screenplay.

00:02:04.890 --> 00:02:06.680
He also decided to
collaborate on the score.

00:02:06.680 --> 00:02:09.380
So, directing duties would
initially fall on Rick Rosenthal,

00:02:09.380 --> 00:02:11.310
but Carpenter didn't like
what he came up with,

00:02:11.310 --> 00:02:12.680
so he reshot some scenes,

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.030
and then he still didn't
like the finished product.

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:17.150
- Listen, Jimmy, I'm not
gonna get into the drama,

00:02:17.150 --> 00:02:19.942
but I am going to get in
to the Things You Missed.

00:02:19.942 --> 00:02:22.442
(eerie music)

00:02:25.400 --> 00:02:26.450
The opening of this film,

00:02:26.450 --> 00:02:29.540
takes us back to that fateful
Halloween night in 1978

00:02:29.540 --> 00:02:31.920
and we fade in backed by
the song "Mr. Sandman,"

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:32.810
by The Chordettes.

00:02:32.810 --> 00:02:35.620
We hear the lyrics, "Mr.
Sandman, bring me a dream."

00:02:35.620 --> 00:02:37.590
This is the first of many
references in this movie

00:02:37.590 --> 00:02:40.080
to the idea of dreams
and the unconscious mind.

00:02:40.080 --> 00:02:42.330
Mr. Sandman is a myth
from European folklore

00:02:42.330 --> 00:02:44.790
and serves as a stand in for
another mythological man,

00:02:44.790 --> 00:02:46.910
The Boogeyman, an alias
that is commonly used

00:02:46.910 --> 00:02:49.560
to refer to Michael
Myers in the first movie.

00:02:49.560 --> 00:02:50.833
- What's the boogeyman?

00:02:51.740 --> 00:02:54.290
- As a matter of fact, it was.

00:02:54.290 --> 00:02:55.687
- The lyric specifically says,

00:02:55.687 --> 00:02:57.810
"Mr. Sandman bring me a dream."

00:02:57.810 --> 00:03:00.540
Throughout the movie, Laurie
is terrified of going to sleep,

00:03:00.540 --> 00:03:02.820
perhaps because part of
her subconscious mind

00:03:02.820 --> 00:03:04.700
knows that her dreams are
where she'll see her brother

00:03:04.700 --> 00:03:06.480
and nemesis, Michael Myers.

00:03:06.480 --> 00:03:08.190
We do see a glimpse of one of those dreams

00:03:08.190 --> 00:03:10.280
when she dozes off at one
point at the hospital,

00:03:10.280 --> 00:03:12.290
though it's unclear if
she made the connection

00:03:12.290 --> 00:03:14.710
about who this mysterious boy actually is,

00:03:14.710 --> 00:03:16.690
but one way or another,
she seems terrified

00:03:16.690 --> 00:03:18.680
of the idea of seeing
the backs of her eyelids

00:03:18.680 --> 00:03:20.270
ever since her encounter with him.

00:03:20.270 --> 00:03:22.270
It's the first thing she
tells the ambulance EMT

00:03:22.270 --> 00:03:24.470
and she reiterates it for the doctor.

00:03:24.470 --> 00:03:26.000
- No, don't put me to sleep.

00:03:26.000 --> 00:03:27.360
- And there's even a deleted scene

00:03:27.360 --> 00:03:30.575
where she uses sheer willpower
to keep herself awake.

00:03:30.575 --> 00:03:32.345
- Is she awake?

00:03:32.345 --> 00:03:34.675
- Dr. Mixter gave her a double bullet.

00:03:34.675 --> 00:03:36.700
If she can keep her eyes
open, she's made out of steel.

00:03:36.700 --> 00:03:38.490
- She actually falls asleep eyes open

00:03:38.490 --> 00:03:40.210
when the medication fully kicks in,

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.440
a testament to just how much

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:44.200
she wanted to avoid seeing
that dream at all costs.

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:45.033
The beginning of the movie

00:03:45.033 --> 00:03:47.110
takes us back to the final
five minutes of the original,

00:03:47.110 --> 00:03:49.230
with a couple of shots
giving us a new perspective

00:03:49.230 --> 00:03:50.310
on those events.

00:03:50.310 --> 00:03:52.340
- In a way, this was a
taste of things to come.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.100
Rick Rosenthal would go to extreme lengths

00:03:54.100 --> 00:03:55.580
to shoot this movie in the same style

00:03:55.580 --> 00:03:56.960
as John Carpenter's Halloween,

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.070
and what better warmup exercise is there

00:03:59.070 --> 00:04:01.500
than adding shots to an
already existing scene.

00:04:01.500 --> 00:04:04.010
This is followed by a long
POV shot of Michael Myers

00:04:04.010 --> 00:04:05.010
spying on the neighborhood,

00:04:05.010 --> 00:04:07.700
which is, of course, a direct
callback to the intro shot

00:04:07.700 --> 00:04:08.860
of the original Halloween,

00:04:08.860 --> 00:04:11.340
where we see the world through
young Michael Myers's eyes.

00:04:11.340 --> 00:04:13.290
Both scenes feature him
looking through windows,

00:04:13.290 --> 00:04:15.360
sneaking inside and then stealing a knife,

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.800
all while no one seems to
notice that he's there.

00:04:17.800 --> 00:04:20.250
Both scenes are also important
from a filmmaking standpoint,

00:04:20.250 --> 00:04:22.610
because one-takes are
notoriously expensive

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.040
and challenging to get right.

00:04:24.040 --> 00:04:25.620
So, a little movie like Halloween II

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:27.090
nailing it as hard as it does,

00:04:27.090 --> 00:04:28.400
really helped it to stand out

00:04:28.400 --> 00:04:30.080
from the growing pool of competitors.

00:04:30.080 --> 00:04:32.330
And that pool was growing
extremely quickly.

00:04:32.330 --> 00:04:35.030
- [Zac] Michael looks in on
Mr. and Mrs. Elrod's house.

00:04:35.030 --> 00:04:36.540
- No, not that Mrs. Elrod.

00:04:36.540 --> 00:04:37.650
- Mr. Elrod is watching

00:04:37.650 --> 00:04:39.480
Dr. Dementia's Horror Movie Marathon,

00:04:39.480 --> 00:04:41.170
a continuation of what Lindsay Wallace

00:04:41.170 --> 00:04:43.290
and Tommy Doyle were seen
watching in the first one.

00:04:43.290 --> 00:04:45.630
We continually see this on
TVs throughout the night,

00:04:45.630 --> 00:04:47.770
whether it's George Romero's
Night of the Living Dead

00:04:47.770 --> 00:04:50.090
or the 1955 horror film, Dementia,

00:04:50.090 --> 00:04:52.380
a movie involving a woman
caught in a nightmare cycle

00:04:52.380 --> 00:04:54.350
that deals with the horror
of the subconscious,

00:04:54.350 --> 00:04:56.040
kind of like how Laurie
feels that she's trapped

00:04:56.040 --> 00:04:57.740
in an inescapable nightmare with Michael,

00:04:57.740 --> 00:04:58.860
which affects her so badly

00:04:58.860 --> 00:05:01.380
she's mortified at the
idea of going to sleep.

00:05:01.380 --> 00:05:04.113
- [Eerie Voice] Now, back to Dementia.

00:05:05.050 --> 00:05:06.450
- When I covered the original movie,

00:05:06.450 --> 00:05:08.790
one symbol that I pointed
out time and time again

00:05:08.790 --> 00:05:10.200
was the use of bedsheets.

00:05:10.200 --> 00:05:12.160
We were always seeing
Michael through these sheets

00:05:12.160 --> 00:05:14.030
and I interpreted that
as part of this metaphor

00:05:14.030 --> 00:05:15.730
for his tendency to go after these teens

00:05:15.730 --> 00:05:17.500
that were trying to go
to bed with one another.

00:05:17.500 --> 00:05:18.810
There is one hookup couple

00:05:18.810 --> 00:05:20.430
that Michael takes out in Halloween II,

00:05:20.430 --> 00:05:22.810
which may be the most brutal
scene in the whole franchise,

00:05:22.810 --> 00:05:24.000
but the main theme of this film,

00:05:24.000 --> 00:05:25.350
if I had to pick just one thing,

00:05:25.350 --> 00:05:28.200
is the idea that Michael is
this never-ending bad dream.

00:05:28.200 --> 00:05:30.430
In that sense, the bed
sheet motif still works,

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:32.020
now being given a different meaning.

00:05:32.020 --> 00:05:33.260
It's associated with the dream

00:05:33.260 --> 00:05:35.340
where Laurie discovers that
she's related to Michael,

00:05:35.340 --> 00:05:37.410
an idea that festers in her subconscious,

00:05:37.410 --> 00:05:39.700
but her conscious mind doesn't wanna face.

00:05:39.700 --> 00:05:41.060
- One constant in nearly all

00:05:41.060 --> 00:05:42.890
of John Carpenter's projects from the time

00:05:42.890 --> 00:05:44.960
is the use of the actress, Nancy Loomis.

00:05:44.960 --> 00:05:46.110
She's the woman who played Annie

00:05:46.110 --> 00:05:47.120
in the original Halloween,

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:49.130
but her death in that
movie doesn't stop her

00:05:49.130 --> 00:05:51.040
from appearing a few times in the sequel.

00:05:51.040 --> 00:05:52.370
I'd say chances are pretty slim

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:53.610
you didn't miss when Annie's body

00:05:53.610 --> 00:05:55.960
was brought out of the house
and identified by her father,

00:05:55.960 --> 00:05:58.460
but you probably didn't
notice that she plays Sally,

00:05:58.460 --> 00:05:59.960
the girl on the other end of the phone

00:05:59.960 --> 00:06:02.550
telling her friend about
what's going on in Haddonfield.

00:06:02.550 --> 00:06:04.448
- [Sally] Didn't you hear?

00:06:04.448 --> 00:06:05.560
- Hear about what?

00:06:05.560 --> 00:06:07.920
- [Sally] Some girl got
killed in Haddonfield.

00:06:07.920 --> 00:06:09.110
It's on the radio.

00:06:09.110 --> 00:06:10.310
- While there are still plenty

00:06:10.310 --> 00:06:12.130
of Halloween I callbacks to discuss,

00:06:12.130 --> 00:06:13.500
there's also a potential look

00:06:13.500 --> 00:06:14.900
at the future of the franchise

00:06:14.900 --> 00:06:18.483
in the form of one seemingly
random side character.

00:06:18.483 --> 00:06:21.233
(menacing music)

